the right-handed hitting Hank
Greenberg down in Lakeland, and
the slugging first sacker of the
Tigers missed it by a foot. This
was not surprising in view of the
quality of Cohen’s service. The
ball came up with the wind carry
ing it—a most decided detriment
as pitchers will tell you—and just
as Hankus Pankus swung with in
tent to burst the stitches, the
globule ducked into the capacious
mit of J. Francis “Shanty” Hogan.
Now J. Francis Hogan is a man
of mirth and girth, and he roared
his appreciation of Syd’s pitch un
til they must have heard him
three counties away.
“Look at that pitch,” said
Stanley Raymond Harris. “It
was a honey.”
Stanley Raymond Harris, it
may be said, made a note on a
certain page in his ready refer
ence book, to remember to pitch
Syd Cohen in the operiing game
of the July 4 double-header.
One pitch may not win a pen
nant, as all the world knows, but
it may be a pretty fair indication
of the sort of arm behind the ball.
Cohen has labored on that screw
ball and he has mastered control
of it.
HAS CONFIDENCE
